heses so we treat  it as a group all divided by H okay and  then this is the limit as H approaches  zero now all we're going to do is we're  just going to do some arithmetic and  simplify this down so what we get is x  squared plus two HX plus h squared plus  three minus x squared minus three  because you have to distribute the  negative okay all divided by H now you  can see we've got some cancellation  occurring here we have X Squared's  cancel the threes cancel and we're left  with 2h X plus h squared divided by H we  could factor out an H so we have 2x plus  H divided by H the H is cancel this is  our final result but if we want to take  the limit as H approaches zero we can  substitute 0 in for H here and we're  gonna get two acts so what the 2x means  is that okay if we were to look at this  graph y equals x squared plus three one  two three it's a parabola like this okay  this is a formula for the slope anywhere  along this curve so for example if we go  over here to x equals one and we go up  to the to the function there two times  one is two that means the instantaneous  rate of change here the slope at that  point is going to be to say we put a  negative one we go up to the curve  that's going to have a slope of negative  two so this is the formula you just want  to memorize the difference quotient and  all it is again is just a substitution  you're putting X plus h in for X okay  and simplifying and you're subtracting f  of X which is the original function and  then again simplifying so this has been  a introduction into how to find the  difference quotient and it's  applications in calculus I'll see you in  the next video
 

